      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 SECOT. Publicado por Elsevier España, S.L.U. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Brown tumours are highly vascular lytic bone lesions found in primary and secondary hyperparathyroidism. The brown term is given due to the red-brown colour of the tissue, which is due to the accumulation of hemosiderin. The case is presented of a 29 year-old male with chronic renal failure, who had a mass in the tip of the ring finger after a trauma of 4 months onset, which had increased progressively in size and pain. He was treated surgically, by amputation, with no recurrence 10 months after the surgery."
      },
